00:20:10.270 --> 00:20:14.810
The moon is kept in orbit not because it's pulled to the Earth

00:20:14.810 --> 00:20:18.110
by some mysterious force, but rather because it rolls

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:22.460
along a curve in the space-time fabric that the Earth creates.

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:29.470
With Einstein, space became not only real, but flexible.

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:34.050
So suddenly space had properties, suddenly space had curvature,

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:40.130
suddenly space had a flexible kind of geometry, almost like a rubber sheet.

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:48.820
It opens up a whole new way of thinking about reality that described the entire universe.

00:20:50.510 --> 00:20:53.910
Einstein becomes Einstein because of that observation.

00:20:55.170 --> 00:20:59.630
Where Newton saw space as passive, Einstein saw it as dynamic.

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:03.660
interwoven with time and it dictates how things move.

00:21:04.310 --> 00:21:09.750
So, after Einstein, space can no longer be thought of as a static stage.

00:21:10.310 --> 00:21:14.710
It's an actor and it plays a leading role in the cosmic drama.

00:21:22.580 --> 00:21:27.820
Now, it's one thing to think of space as dynamic, active and flexible, like a fabric.

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:29.550
But is it really?

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:34.810
Is this just a metaphor, or does it actually describe what space is?

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:39.600
Well, Einstein's theory predicts that one way to find out

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:43.540
would be to take a little journey to the edge of a black hole.

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:47.500
Black holes are collapsed stars,

00:21:48.190 --> 00:21:52.230
massive objects crushed to a fraction of their original size.

00:21:53.190 --> 00:21:57.340
Gravity around them is so strong that, according to Einstein's math,

00:21:57.820 --> 00:21:59.780
a spinning black hole can literally...

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.590
drag space along with it, twisting it

00:22:03.590 --> 00:22:05.350
like an actual piece of cloth.

00:22:06.350 --> 00:22:09.250
The nearest black hole is trillions of miles away,

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:12.090
making it a challenge to test this prediction.

00:22:13.180 --> 00:22:16.980
But in the late 1950s, a physicist named Leonard Schiff

00:22:17.070 --> 00:22:20.330
began searching for a way to test Einstein's ideas

00:22:20.330 --> 00:22:22.510
about space much closer to home.

00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:26.930
Schiff was inspired by something we usually think of

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:29.910
as a child's toy, a gyroscope.

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:35.520
He thought that if space really twists, like a fabric,

00:22:36.450 --> 00:22:39.030
a gyroscope might allow him to detect it.

00:22:40.540 --> 00:22:45.340
It was a strange idea, and he chose a strange place to share it with the world.

00:22:48.420 --> 00:22:50.340
Faculty Swimming Pool at Stanford.

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:56.470
Here, in 1959, Schiff met with two colleagues,

00:22:56.720 --> 00:22:58.460
William Fairbank and Bob Cannon.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.380
excited about an ad he'd seen for a high-tech gyroscope.

00:23:03.860 --> 00:23:07.320
Though it looked different, it basically worked the same as the child's toy.

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:13.950
Then and there, the three decided to launch a device like this into orbit around the Earth.

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:19.300
Normally a gyroscope's axis points in a fixed direction,

00:23:20.210 --> 00:23:22.830
but if Earth is actually dragging space,

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:26.100
then the gyroscope's axis would be dragged along with it,

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:29.440
shifting its orientation in a way that could be measured.

00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:33.120
It was a brilliantly simple plan.

00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:35.500
There was just one problem.

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:39.820
Einstein's theories predict that the Earth's rotation

00:23:39.820 --> 00:23:44.900
twists space by only a tiny amount, an amount so small

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:47.440
it would be like trying to measure the height of a penny

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:50.090
from 62 miles away.

00:23:53.390 --> 00:23:56.610
The team spent more than two years trying to figure out

00:23:56.610 --> 00:23:58.830
how to make such a precise measurement.

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:04.330
finally devised a plan to attach four freely floating gyroscopes

00:24:04.330 --> 00:24:06.970
to a telescope aimed at a distant star.

00:24:08.100 --> 00:24:11.740
If space twists, then over time, the gyroscopes

00:24:11.740 --> 00:24:13.620
would no longer point at the star

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:16.680
since they'd get caught up in the swirl of space.

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:21.510
And in 1962, they applied to NASA for a grant,

00:24:21.970 --> 00:24:24.010
requesting around a million dollars

00:24:24.010 --> 00:24:27.740
for what would come to be called Gravity Probe B.

00:24:30.060 --> 00:24:33.860
Members of the team originally thought the project would take about three years.

00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:36.780
They were just a little optimistic.

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:43.090
With an ever-growing team, Gravity Pro-B became one of the longest-running experiments in history.

00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:48.150
Decade after decade was spent trying to realize the original vision,

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:53.280
which meant launching a telescope into space and building gyroscopes

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:56.590
that were among the smoothest objects ever created.

00:24:57.670 --> 00:24:59.090
Technology is just frightening.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.060
It was like the carrot on the front of the mule.

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:06.140
It was like it was always five to ten years away when we could do this.

00:25:07.020 --> 00:25:10.100
And it was five to ten years away for about 35 years.

00:25:11.690 --> 00:25:18.170
Consuming more than four decades and $750 million, the project was nearly cancelled

00:25:18.170 --> 00:25:20.190
by NASA nine times.

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:23.630
Ten, nine, eight, eight, nine.

00:25:23.630 --> 00:25:28.880
Finally, April of 2004, the team gathered to witness the launch.

00:25:33.200 --> 00:25:41.040
Of the three men who sat by the pool back in 1959,

00:25:41.560 --> 00:25:43.780
only one was alive to see it.

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:45.800
And there we were, watching.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:49.180
It's a terribly exciting moment in your life.

00:25:50.570 --> 00:25:52.590
Just a thrilling experience.

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:56.220
It was flawless.

00:25:56.760 --> 00:25:59.180
10,000 things did not go wrong.

00:26:01.310 --> 00:26:04.330
For over a year, Gravity Probe B orbited the Earth

00:26:04.330 --> 00:26:07.670
while the team nervously monitored its every move,

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:11.610
trying to see if the Earth would actually twist space.

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:18.180
Finally, the data began to trickle in, and there was a problem.

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:23.380
The gyroscopes were experiencing a tiny, unexpected wobble,

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:26.220
and to clean up the data would cost millions.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:37.200
With funds running out, it looked like nearly half a century of work was about to go down the drain.

00:26:40.860 --> 00:26:45.750
Then, at almost the last possible moment, two sources of additional funding emerged.

00:26:45.770 --> 00:26:51.260
The son of original team leader William Fairbank, who made a private donation,

00:26:52.220 --> 00:26:57.730
and Turkey al-Sahd, a member of the Saudi royal family with a degree in aeronautics from Stanford,

00:26:58.360 --> 00:26:59.980
who arranged for a large grant.

00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:05.360
Over the next two years, the problem with the data was solved,

00:27:05.800 --> 00:27:12.760
revealing that the axes of the gyroscope shifted by almost exactly the amount predicted by Einstein's equations.

00:27:14.210 --> 00:27:21.750
I think it's the first time that you can actually see Einstein's effect, his drift, with the naked eye.

00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:29.980
This experiment provides the most direct evidence ever found that space is...

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:33.970
is something real, a physical entity like a fabric.

00:27:35.590 --> 00:27:37.910
After all, if space were nothing,

00:27:38.640 --> 00:27:40.280
there would be nothing to twist.

00:27:44.770 --> 00:27:47.370
But at the same time that Albert Einstein

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:49.930
was investigating space on the largest of scales,

00:27:50.270 --> 00:27:53.390
another band of physicists was probing the universe

00:27:53.390 --> 00:27:55.440
on extremely tiny scales.

00:27:56.060 --> 00:27:59.980
And there they found a completely uncharted realm, right?

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:03.750
Einstein's picture of space, it was nowhere to be found.

00:28:04.130 --> 00:28:05.470
Do you see what I'm talking about?

00:28:05.990 --> 00:28:08.330
Imagine you could shrink billions of times

00:28:08.330 --> 00:28:09.990
smaller than your current size.

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:16.540
This is the realm of atoms and subatomic particles,

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:19.900
the fundamental building blocks of everything we can see.

00:28:20.920 --> 00:28:22.680
And when you get down to this size,

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:25.980
the world plays by a wildly different set of rules

00:28:25.980 --> 00:28:27.340
called quantum mechanics.

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:29.980
According to these rules, even

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:33.180
Even if you try to remove every last atom and particle,

00:28:33.500 --> 00:28:36.980
you'd find that empty space is still far from empty.

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:40.450
In fact, it's teeming with activity.

00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:45.430
Particles are constantly popping in and out of existence.

00:28:45.830 --> 00:28:48.290
They erupt out of nothingness,

00:28:48.620 --> 00:28:50.800
quickly annihilate each other, and disappear.

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:55.850
In quantum mechanics, empty space is not that empty.

00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:57.610
It's full of fluctuating fields,

00:28:57.810 --> 00:28:59.970
full of all sorts of jittery things.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:04.340
going on. It's a place where particles are constantly fluctuating and

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:08.640
annihilating each other and being created again and annihilating. It's a place of

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:14.700
chaos and bubbling. While the theory predicted this, it wasn't until 1948

00:29:14.700 --> 00:29:20.080
that a scientist named Hendrik Casimir suggested that even though we can't see

00:29:20.080 --> 00:29:25.680
these particles, they should cause empty space to do something we can see. He

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:29.410
He predicted that if you take two ordinary metal plates,

00:29:32.560 --> 00:29:35.600
and place them extremely close together,

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:39.320
say, closer together than the thickness of a sheet of paper,

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:41.670
then particles with certain energies

00:29:41.670 --> 00:29:44.070
would be excluded because, in some sense,

00:29:44.560 --> 00:29:46.920
they wouldn't fit between the plates.

00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:51.540
With more of this frenetic activity outside the plates

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:54.290
than inside, Casimir thought the plates

00:29:54.290 --> 00:29:57.770
would be pushed together by what we usually think of

00:29:57.800 --> 00:29:59.000
as empty space.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.260
And some years later, when the experiment was done,

00:30:09.980 --> 00:30:11.620
Azamir was proven right.

00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:14.540
In empty space, the plates were pushed together.

00:30:14.840 --> 00:30:18.930
So on atomic scales, empty space is not empty.

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:21.140
It's so flooded with activity

00:30:21.140 --> 00:30:23.760
that it can force objects to move.

00:30:26.190 --> 00:30:28.710
And today, the quest to understand space

00:30:28.710 --> 00:30:29.970
on the smallest scale is...

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:32.670
continuing with one of the most expensive

00:30:32.670 --> 00:30:34.910
science experiments in history.

00:30:36.720 --> 00:30:39.580
This is CERN, the European Organization

00:30:39.580 --> 00:30:41.600
for Nuclear Research in Geneva.

00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:45.280
And here, buried a few hundred feet below the ground,

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:47.580
is the Large Hadron Collider,

00:30:47.900 --> 00:30:50.400
the world's most powerful accelerator.

00:30:50.960 --> 00:30:53.020
With a price tag of about $10 billion,

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:55.920
it accelerates subatomic particles

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:59.980
to more than 99.99% of the speed of light.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:02.570
light and smashes them into each other.

00:31:02.970 --> 00:31:05.710
In the showers of debris produced by these collisions,

00:31:05.950 --> 00:31:09.710
scientists at places like this have discovered a whole zoo

00:31:09.710 --> 00:31:11.930
of strange and exotic particles.

00:31:12.300 --> 00:31:16.190
And right now, they're chasing one of the most elusive,

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:20.540
a particle thought to be essential to shaping everything

00:31:20.540 --> 00:31:25.710
from the atoms in our bodies to the most distant stars.

00:31:27.910 --> 00:31:29.590
If this particle is found,

00:31:30.120 --> 00:31:36.210
It will redefine our picture of space and fulfill a quest begun more than 40 years ago.

00:31:36.670 --> 00:31:47.340
It all started in 1964 when a young English physicist named Peter Higgs suggested something

00:31:47.340 --> 00:31:56.400
about space that was so radical it nearly ruined him. I was told that I was talking nonsense,

00:31:56.400 --> 00:31:58.490
that I couldn't be right.

00:31:59.410 --> 00:31:59.970
So...

00:32:00.060 --> 00:32:03.020
They clearly hadn't understood what I was saying.

00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:10.000
Higgs and a few others were wrestling with a puzzle which comes down to this.

00:32:10.820 --> 00:32:15.200
The fundamental particles in the universe all contain different amounts of mass,

00:32:15.490 --> 00:32:17.750
which we usually think of as weight.

00:32:19.890 --> 00:32:25.360
Without mass, these particles would never combine to form the familiar atoms

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:28.800
that make up all the stuff we see in the world around us.

00:32:30.640 --> 00:32:35.580
But what creates mass, and why do different particles have different masses?

00:32:37.420 --> 00:32:41.990
Try as they might, no one had been able to answer this perplexing question.

00:32:43.100 --> 00:32:48.800
Then one weekend, after a walk outside Edinburgh, Higgs had a peculiar idea.

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:53.540
Using mathematics, he imagines space in a new way.

00:32:54.060 --> 00:32:59.900
As something like an ocean, particles are immersed in this ocean and gain mass,

00:33:00.060 --> 00:33:01.080
as they move through it.

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:05.600
To see how this works, think of a particle's mass

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:07.160
like an actor's fame.

00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:10.590
And the Higgs Ocean is like the paparazzi.

00:33:12.190 --> 00:33:16.240
Some particles, like unknown actors, pass through with ease.

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:19.240
The paparazzi simply aren't interested in them.

00:33:20.140 --> 00:33:22.560
But other particles, like superstars,

00:33:22.920 --> 00:33:24.540
have to push and press.

00:33:25.140 --> 00:33:28.520
And the more those particles struggle to get through,

00:33:28.890 --> 00:33:29.970
the more they interact with them.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:38.780
with the ocean and the more mass they gain. Higgs was convinced he'd made a great discovery,

00:33:39.950 --> 00:33:49.560
but when he submitted his idea to a journal at CERN, it was rejected. Undaunted, Higgs honed his

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:55.840
theory further, until he was offered the chance to present it at Einstein's Old Haunt,

00:33:55.980 --> 00:33:58.420
the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:04.450
There, he expected his new idea would meet some of its toughest critics.

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:12.050
I was happily driving up the freeway, and then there was a sign to turn off for Princeton.

00:34:12.230 --> 00:34:14.970
And that really confronted me with what I was going into.

00:34:15.620 --> 00:34:22.100
I broke out in a cold sweat and started trembling, and I had to pull off the road to recover.

00:34:24.010 --> 00:34:25.370
But Higgs persevered.

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:29.970
It was the first in a series of talks that would convince colleagues far more.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:37.870
and wide that he was onto something profound eventually i i sort of wore them down i felt i

