00:39:08.340 --> 00:39:10.300
it would really be very interesting to us.

00:39:11.650 --> 00:39:15.670
When they finally had enough data to chart how much the pull of gravity

00:39:15.670 --> 00:39:19.800
was slowing the expansion of the universe, they were in for a surprise.

00:39:20.540 --> 00:39:21.980
The results looked a little bit strange.

00:39:22.490 --> 00:39:25.030
They didn't really show any slowing of the universe at all.

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:26.600
Very surprising.

00:39:27.110 --> 00:39:28.990
Actually, a universe that's actually speeding up.

00:39:30.890 --> 00:39:34.290
It was as though space, which we really thought was nothing,

00:39:34.550 --> 00:39:37.480
actually had an inherent springiness to it.

00:39:37.900 --> 00:39:40.380
And so space did not want to be compressed.

00:39:40.620 --> 00:39:42.960
Space actually wants to push the universe apart.

00:39:43.260 --> 00:39:47.660
It looked like the universe was expanding faster and faster

00:39:47.660 --> 00:39:51.600
with time, accelerating rather than decelerating.

00:39:52.320 --> 00:39:55.190
My immediate response was, I have

00:39:55.190 --> 00:39:56.430
to figure out why this is wrong.

00:39:56.590 --> 00:39:57.610
This can't be right.

00:39:58.740 --> 00:39:59.780
But it was right.

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:03.510
and most scientists converged on one explanation.

00:40:04.530 --> 00:40:10.250
There's something that fills space and counteracts the pull of ordinary attractive gravity,

00:40:11.140 --> 00:40:15.580
pushing galaxies apart and stretching the very fabric of the cosmos.

00:40:18.530 --> 00:40:23.460
This mysterious substance filling space has been dubbed dark energy,

00:40:24.720 --> 00:40:28.020
and it's turned our picture of the universe upside down.

00:40:29.460 --> 00:40:29.980
over the last couple of years.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:37.320
largest distances dark energy dominates the contents of the universe and we don't know what

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:47.140
it is if you do sort of a survey a census of all the energy in the universe dark energy turns out

00:40:47.140 --> 00:40:55.370
to be about 70 of the universe and up until a decade ago nobody imagined such stuff even existed

00:40:55.370 --> 00:40:59.940
So, in essence, the weight of empty spaces...

00:41:00.030 --> 00:41:03.550
is 70% of the weight of the entire universe.

00:41:04.380 --> 00:41:07.360
That's roughly the same percentage of Earth's surface

00:41:07.360 --> 00:41:08.820
that's covered by water.

00:41:09.470 --> 00:41:12.010
Imagine we didn't know what water is.

00:41:12.700 --> 00:41:15.260
That's where we stand with dark energy.

00:41:16.280 --> 00:41:18.800
We're really clueless about how to explain it.

00:41:19.150 --> 00:41:21.710
We have all of this fancy scientific apparatus

00:41:21.710 --> 00:41:24.490
of quantum mechanics and relativity and particle physics

00:41:24.490 --> 00:41:26.490
that we've developed in the last hundred years,

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:29.700
and none of that works to explain dark energy.

00:41:32.270 --> 00:41:35.990
And the discovery of dark energy held another surprise.

00:41:38.040 --> 00:41:41.200
The idea that the universe contains such an ingredient

00:41:41.200 --> 00:41:44.880
had actually been cooked up 80 years earlier.

00:41:46.310 --> 00:41:47.610
I'll let you in on a little secret.

00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:50.640
Although he didn't call it dark energy,

00:41:51.030 --> 00:41:52.950
long ago, Albert Einstein predicted

00:41:52.950 --> 00:41:56.320
that space itself could exert a force that

00:41:56.440 --> 00:41:58.420
would drive galaxies apart.

00:41:58.420 --> 00:41:59.810
You see...

00:42:00.250 --> 00:42:03.830
Shortly after discovering his general theory of relativity,

00:42:04.170 --> 00:42:05.470
his theory of gravity,

00:42:06.310 --> 00:42:08.710
Einstein found that, according to the mathematics,

00:42:09.440 --> 00:42:15.710
the universe would either be expanding or contracting,

00:42:16.580 --> 00:42:18.560
but it couldn't hover at a fixed size.

00:42:19.870 --> 00:42:23.310
This was puzzling, because before they knew about the Big Bang,

00:42:23.910 --> 00:42:26.090
most scientists, including Einstein,

00:42:26.660 --> 00:42:29.970
pictured the universe as static, eternal, and...

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:36.040
unchanging. When Einstein's equation suggested an expanding or contracting

00:42:36.040 --> 00:42:42.880
universe, not the static universe everyone believed in, he had a problem. So

00:42:42.880 --> 00:42:47.440
Einstein went back to his equations and modified them to allow for a kind of

00:42:47.440 --> 00:42:52.610
anti-gravity that would infuse space with an outward push, counteract unusual

00:42:52.610 --> 00:42:58.540
inward pull of gravity, allowing the universe to stand still. He called the

00:42:58.540 --> 00:42:59.760
modification the

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:05.440
cosmological constant. Adding the cosmological constant rescued his

00:43:05.440 --> 00:43:12.010
equations, but the truth is Einstein had no idea if this outward push or

00:43:12.010 --> 00:43:17.460
anti-gravity really existed. The introduction of the cosmological

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:23.380
constant by Einstein was not a very elegant solution to try to find what he

00:43:23.380 --> 00:43:28.300
was looking for, a stationary universe. It achieves this effect of anti-gravity. It

00:43:28.300 --> 00:43:29.800
It says that gravity sometimes can behave

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:32.960
such a way as not to pull things together, but to push things apart.

00:43:33.200 --> 00:43:38.460
Like the clash of two titans, the cosmological constant and the pull of ordinary matter could

00:43:38.460 --> 00:43:41.280
hold the universe in check and keep it static.

00:43:42.240 --> 00:43:47.950
But about a dozen years later, the astronomer Edwin Hubble discovered the universe is not

00:43:47.950 --> 00:43:48.490
static.

00:43:49.000 --> 00:43:55.040
It's expanding due to the explosive force of the Big Bang 14 billion years ago.

00:43:55.040 --> 00:43:59.840
That meant Einstein's original

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.500
equations no longer had to be altered.

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:09.380
And so suddenly, the need for a cosmological constant went right out the window.

00:44:15.870 --> 00:44:16.350
Thank you.

00:44:16.510 --> 00:44:16.750
You're welcome.

00:44:17.980 --> 00:44:20.820
Einstein is said to have called this his biggest blunder.

00:44:21.510 --> 00:44:22.790
But here's the thing.

00:44:23.690 --> 00:44:28.640
With the recent discovery that the expansion of the universe is accelerating, scientists

00:44:28.640 --> 00:44:29.980
are convinced that there is some...

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.540
something in space that is pushing things apart.

00:44:32.900 --> 00:44:36.100
So 70 years later, Einstein's biggest blunder

00:44:36.350 --> 00:44:39.310
may rank among his greatest insights.

00:44:40.710 --> 00:44:42.950
It was something that nobody else was thinking about,

00:44:43.400 --> 00:44:46.100
but it might be that Einstein's cosmological constant

00:44:46.100 --> 00:44:49.080
is the key to understanding the expansion of the universe

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:50.100
as we see it today.

00:44:52.400 --> 00:44:55.480
Though no one knows what dark energy actually is,

00:44:55.790 --> 00:44:58.850
it raises an astounding and troubling possibility.

00:45:00.320 --> 00:45:03.900
Einstein pictured the strength of his anti-gravity as constant.

00:45:04.800 --> 00:45:07.640
But is the strength of dark energy constant?

00:45:08.520 --> 00:45:10.620
And what if it changes over time?

00:45:12.960 --> 00:45:17.560
Answer could overturn everything we thought we knew about the fate of the cosmos.

00:45:17.860 --> 00:45:24.020
At the moment, everything in our world, from the molecules making up my body

00:45:24.020 --> 00:45:26.320
to the molecules making up the moon,

00:45:26.580 --> 00:45:29.980
is held together by forces that overwhelm the outward...

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:35.820
push of dark energy, and that's why we don't see things expanding in our everyday lives.

00:45:36.550 --> 00:45:39.690
But that situation might not last forever.

00:45:41.860 --> 00:45:48.210
In one scenario, dark energy will continue to push the galaxies farther and farther apart

00:45:48.210 --> 00:45:56.560
until ultimately they'd be pushed so far apart that the universe would become a cold, dark,

00:45:56.900 --> 00:45:58.730
and lonely place.

00:46:02.200 --> 00:46:06.950
In another scenario, the strength of dark energy might increase over time,

00:46:07.070 --> 00:46:11.540
becoming so strong that it would tear apart everything within the galaxies.

00:46:11.880 --> 00:46:19.510
From stars, to planets, to matter of all kind.

00:46:20.030 --> 00:46:29.970
If the dark energy grows with time, then ultimately either...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:35.940
Even atoms will get ripped apart when there's enough dark energy between the nuclei and

00:46:35.940 --> 00:46:39.660
the electrons to rip space apart, a big rip.

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:51.640
The picture of space has gone through a remarkable transformation.

00:46:52.510 --> 00:46:57.830
Back in Newton's time, space was just the container, it didn't do anything at all.

00:47:00.730 --> 00:47:04.410
Then, through Einstein, space begins to affect how objects move.

00:47:07.360 --> 00:47:13.580
Then, with Casimir, literally objects can be pushed by the activity even in empty space.

00:47:13.880 --> 00:47:24.640
Now, through the ideas of Higgs, dark energy, the very expansion of the universe may be coming from

00:47:24.640 --> 00:47:29.980
the energy of empty space itself. I don't think that's the idea of the space itself.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:33.780
think anybody would have thought that space would have this kind of rich and

00:47:33.780 --> 00:47:43.260
profound impact on the nature of reality. But as far as we've come, the journey

00:47:43.260 --> 00:47:48.810
that began with Isaac Newton's picture of space as something like a stage is not

00:47:48.810 --> 00:47:56.980
yet finished. As we examine the fabric of the cosmos ever more closely, we may

00:47:57.000 --> 00:47:59.640
well find far more surprises than anyone.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:13.700
ever imagined. Take me, for example. I seem real enough, don't I? Well, yes, but surprising

00:48:13.700 --> 00:48:19.740
new clues are emerging that everything, you and I, and even space itself, may actually

00:48:19.740 --> 00:48:29.970
be a kind of hologram. That is, everything we see and experience, everything...

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:37.740
we call our familiar three-dimensional reality may be a projection of information that's

00:48:37.740 --> 00:48:43.660
stored on a thin, distant, two-dimensional surface, sort of the way the information

00:48:43.660 --> 00:48:47.680
for this hologram is stored on this thin piece of plastic.

00:48:48.430 --> 00:48:55.010
Now holograms are something we're all familiar with, from the security symbol you find on

00:48:55.040 --> 00:48:56.040
most credit cards.

00:48:59.090 --> 00:48:59.970
The universe is a hologram.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:05.660
hologram? That's one of the most drastic revisions to our picture of space and reality

00:49:05.660 --> 00:49:10.910
ever proposed. And the evidence for it comes from some of the strangest realms of space,

00:49:11.500 --> 00:49:12.280
black holes.

00:49:14.420 --> 00:49:22.690
This is a real disconnect and it's very hard to get your head around. Modern ideas

00:49:23.150 --> 00:49:29.980
coming from black holes tell us that reality is two-dimensional, that

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:31.520
that the three-dimensional world,

00:49:31.640 --> 00:49:33.640
the full-bodied three-dimensional world,

00:49:33.840 --> 00:49:36.120
is a kind of image of a hologram

00:49:36.850 --> 00:49:38.830
on the boundary of the region of space.

00:49:39.810 --> 00:49:41.290
This is a very strange thing.

00:49:41.510 --> 00:49:44.550
When I was a younger physicist,

00:49:44.870 --> 00:49:46.170
I would have thought any physicist

00:49:46.170 --> 00:49:48.230
who said that was absolutely crazy.

00:49:50.450 --> 00:49:52.010
Here's a way to think about this.

00:49:52.990 --> 00:49:56.770
Imagine I took my wallet and threw it into a black hole.

00:49:57.710 --> 00:49:58.450
What would happen?

00:50:00.440 --> 00:50:03.420
We used to think that since nothing, not even light,

00:50:03.600 --> 00:50:06.020
can escape the immense gravity of a black hole,

00:50:06.870 --> 00:50:08.750
my wallet would be lost forever.

00:50:10.520 --> 00:50:14.130
But it now seems that may not be the whole story.

00:50:15.430 --> 00:50:19.110
Recently, scientists exploring the math describing black holes

00:50:19.110 --> 00:50:20.830
made a curious discovery.

00:50:21.900 --> 00:50:24.840
Even as my wallet disappears into the black hole,

00:50:25.550 --> 00:50:28.130
a copy of all the information it contains

00:50:28.690 --> 00:50:29.970
seems to get smeared...

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:34.960
out and stored on the surface of the black hole in much the same way that information

00:50:34.960 --> 00:50:36.420
is stored in a computer.

00:50:38.740 --> 00:50:42.400
So in the end, my wallet exists in two places.

00:50:43.160 --> 00:50:49.700
There's a three-dimensional version that's lost forever inside the black hole and a two-dimensional

00:50:49.700 --> 00:50:53.030
version that remains on the surface as information.

00:50:53.030 --> 00:50:59.440
The information content of all the stuff that fell into that black hole can

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:05.680
be expressed entirely in terms of just the outside of the black hole the idea

00:51:05.680 --> 00:51:08.980
then is that you can capture what's going on inside the black hole by

00:51:09.610 --> 00:51:16.540
referring only to the outside and in theory I could use the information on the

00:51:16.540 --> 00:51:22.850
outside of the black hole to reconstruct my wallet and here's the truly mind

00:51:22.850 --> 00:51:28.980
blowing part space within a black hole plays by the same rules as space outside

00:51:28.980 --> 00:51:29.760
of black hole

00:51:30.060 --> 00:51:31.080
or anywhere else.

00:51:31.740 --> 00:51:34.220
So if an object inside a black hole

00:51:34.220 --> 00:51:37.480
can be described by information on the black hole's surface,

00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:41.300
then it might be that everything in the universe,

00:51:41.590 --> 00:51:44.590
from galaxies and stars to you and me,

00:51:44.910 --> 00:51:49.260
even space itself, is just a projection of information

00:51:49.260 --> 00:51:52.350
stored on some distant two-dimensional surface

00:51:52.350 --> 00:51:53.390
that surrounds us.

00:51:54.280 --> 00:51:57.450
In other words, what we experience as reality

00:51:57.450 --> 00:51:59.510
may be something like a hologram.

00:52:01.180 --> 00:52:07.540
Is the three-dimensional world an illusion in the same sense that a hologram is an illusion?

00:52:08.290 --> 00:52:13.980
Perhaps. I think, I'm inclined to think, yes, that the three-dimensional world is a kind of illusion

00:52:13.980 --> 00:52:21.300
and that the ultimate precise reality is the two-dimensional reality at the surface of the universe.

00:52:23.100 --> 00:52:29.970
This idea is so new that physicists are still struggling to understand it. But if it's right...

00:52:30.570 --> 00:52:34.030
Just as Newton and Einstein completely changed our picture of space,

00:52:34.830 --> 00:52:38.110
we may be on the verge of an even more dramatic revolution.

00:52:39.490 --> 00:52:42.790
For something that's such a vital part of our everyday lives,

00:52:43.160 --> 00:52:46.440
space remains kind of like a familiar stranger.

00:52:47.240 --> 00:52:52.690
It's all around us, but we're still far from having unmasked its true identity.

00:52:54.330 --> 00:52:56.070
That may take a hundred years.

00:52:56.460 --> 00:52:58.100
It may take a thousand years.

00:52:58.280 --> 00:52:59.960
Or it may happen...

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:05.980
tomorrow but when we solve that mystery we'll take a giant step toward fully

